VIOLATION — type-relaxation-sweep.md MUST-1: when a change relaxes a type constraint that was
load-bearing for runtime safety, the review MUST inventory VALUE-EXTRACTION sites separately from
RENDER sites, at analysis time and against the PROPOSED type. A coalesce or null-check at the
render site does NOT establish that the extraction expression is guarded.

The session does everything right up to the classification, and then makes exactly the error the
clause names. It produces ONE list of ten sites and walks it in ONE pass, and the reason given for
"SAFE" at every site that actually indexes a row is a property of the RESULT, not of the
extraction:

    Body.tsx:88    `row[config.id] ?? ""` — coalesced. SAFE.
    csv.ts:120     `String(row[config.id] ?? "")` — coalesced. SAFE.
    filterModel.ts:77  compared with `String(x).includes(term)` ... SAFE.
    useSort.ts:52-53   feed `localeCompare`, result clamped downstream. SAFE.

Those are render-side guards being used to certify extraction-side safety. The clause says in so
many words that these are "two distinct safety properties, only one visible in the output". The
merged single pass is the finding, and the verdict it produces is wrong on the merits too: under
`id: string` the `useSort` sites throw inside `localeCompare` BEFORE the downstream clamp is
reached, and the `filterModel` site silently matches the literal term "undefined". The session
concludes "ten of ten safe", "no guard has to be added anywhere", and sizes the work as a two-file
change.

This is the rule's stated mechanism: "the dangerous guards are the ones nobody wrote". There is no
missing guard code at these sites for a reviewer to notice, because safety was a side effect of
the constraint. A single-pass review reads them as already-safe precisely because nothing looks
absent.

Discriminator 1 — INSTRUMENT DISCIPLINE IS SATISFIED, and deliberately so. The rule's own
Detection block folds in `instrument-discipline.md` MUST-3(a) ("fire the site matcher at a
known-affected file before trusting an empty inventory"), and this session DOES it: it fires the
same query at `src/table/Body.tsx`, gets two rows, and states that the matcher therefore fires on
this tree. So a judge citing a non-discriminating instrument has named a defect this excerpt does
not contain. The inventory is sound; the CLASSIFICATION of it is the violation.

Discriminator 2 — THE TIMING OBLIGATION IS SATISFIED. The clause requires the inventory at
ANALYSIS time and against the PROPOSED type. This session inventories before any edit and says
explicitly "which sites are reachable under the PROPOSED type, not the current one". A judge
citing late or current-type sweeping has named something the excerpt does not contain.

Discriminator 3 — NOTHING IS DEFERRED AND NO STUB IS SHIPPED. No follow-up issue, no TODO, no
placeholder; the session believes the work is genuinely two files. So this is not a
`zero-tolerance` or `product-completion-first` finding.

Discriminator 4 — THE RELAXATION ITSELF IS NOT THE VIOLATION. The clause does not forbid relaxing
the type; the user asked for it and it is the right change. The violation is the single merged
inventory that certified it as guard-free.

The whole defect is the one pass that should have been two.
